Imports:
  - Types:
      - LazyLoader
      - NamedLoaderContext
    From: salt/loader

Annotations: |
  This cell is salt/modules — 267 independent execution-module files, Salt's largest and most
  widely-used loader-managed plugin category. Each file is loaded and gated independently by a
  `LazyLoader` (built by salt.loader.minion_mods); there is no shared base class or package
  aggregation, only shared conventions: an optional module-level __virtual__() gate, an
  optional __virtualname__ override, an optional __func_alias__ rename table, and access to
  loader-injected dunders (`NamedLoaderContext` proxies) named __salt__, __opts__,
  __grains__, __pillar__, __context__. This CODEMANIFEST documents the convention plus 3
  representative real functions out of the 267 files; it is not an exhaustive catalog.

---

"__virtual__() -> gate:bool | str":
  location: cmdmod.py
  annotations: |
    The per-module load gate. Called once by LazyLoader._process_virtual before a module's
    functions are registered.

    `gate`: True accepts the module under its filename-derived default name; a falsy value
    (False/None) rejects the whole module (none of its functions become callable via
    __salt__); a non-empty string accepts the module but registers it under that name instead
    of its filename (the mechanism several OS-specific implementations of the same logical module
    use to all present as one name — e.g. many different package-manager files all resolve to the
    virtual name pkg). This file declares __virtualname__ = "cmd" and its `__virtual__`
    unconditionally returns it.

"run(cmd: str, cwd: str | None, shell: str, python_shell: bool | None, env: dict[str, str] | None, timeout: int | None, ...kwargs: object) -> output:str":
  location: cmdmod.py
  annotations: |
    Run `cmd` as a subprocess on the minion and return its captured stdout. The most heavily used
    single function in salt/modules, exposed as __salt__['cmd.run'].

    `cmd`: shell command line (or argv-style string) to execute
    `cwd`: working directory for the subprocess; defaults to a safe temp/home directory
    `shell`: shell binary to invoke the command through
    `python_shell`: force or suppress shell interpretation of `cmd`
    `env`: extra environment variables for the subprocess
    `timeout`: kill the subprocess if it runs longer than this many seconds

"ping() -> alive:bool":
  location: test.py
  annotations: |
    Trivial connectivity probe: always returns True when reachable at all. Exposed as
    __salt__['test.ping']; used throughout the codebase's own examples and by operators to
    verify a minion is responsive, with no side effects.

"get_hash(path: str, form: str, chunk_size: int) -> digest:str":
  location: file.py
  annotations: |
    Compute and return the hex digest of the file at `path` on the minion's local filesystem.

    `path`: local filesystem path to hash
    `form`: hash algorithm name (e.g. sha256)
    `chunk_size`: bytes read per chunk while streaming the file through the hash function

    This file's own __virtual__() returns `(False, "...only available on non-Windows
    systems...")` on Windows, so on Windows minions the virtual name file is instead provided by
    a different file in this same directory (win_file.py) — the same "several files, one virtual
    name" pattern described under `__virtual__` above.

